  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Canonical Associativity Bijection for Cartesian Products}",
  "body_text": null,
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven: A, B, C is \\set\nthen:\n. exists: phi(x__) is \\function:on{(.(.A \\.set.cross./ B.) \\.set.cross./ C.)}:to{(.A \\.set.cross./ (.B \\.set.cross./ C.).)}\n  suchThat:\n  . phi is? \\(bijective)::function:?on{(.(.A \\.set.cross./ B.) \\.set.cross./ C.)}:?to{(.A \\.set.cross./ (.B \\.set.cross./ C.).)}\n  . forAll: a \"in\" A\n    then:\n    . forAll: b \"in\" B\n      then:\n      . forAll: c \"in\" C\n        then: phi(((a, b), c)) = (a, (b, c))\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Canonical Associativity Bijection for Cartesian Products\"\n. description: \"The map $((a,b),c)\\mapsto(a,(b,c))$ is a canonical bijection\n                from $(A\\times B)\\times C$ to $A\\times(B\\times C)$. The two\n                products are therefore naturally identifiable but are not\n                literally equal under the ordered-pair encoding.\"\nId: \"cd332b77-e7cc-4e59-92e6-40decc503be8\"",
